      "symbol": "applyTo",
      "line": 23,
      "explanation": "HistoryDelta.applyTo enacts one history entry. It applies the element delta with version and versionNonce excluded, passing the previous store snapshot's elements as the fallback map so elements missing from the current scene can still be replayed; then it applies the app-state delta to the resulting elements and returns the OR of the element-visible-change and app-state-visible-change answers. This is the history-entry variant, distinct from StoreDelta.applyTo, which falls back to StoreSnapshot.empty().elements."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/delta.ts",
      "symbol": "createApplier",
      "line": 1666,
      "explanation": "ElementsDelta.createApplier is the factory returning the per-bucket closure used for added, removed, and updated deltas. It resolves each element through createGetter, applies its patch, and, at the first element for which a previous element exists, infers replay direction by comparing revision numbers: prevElement.version > nextElement.version means backward, otherwise forward. If no comparable previous element is found, direction stays undefined and resolveConflicts defaults to forward."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/delta.ts",
      "symbol": "resolveConflicts",
      "line": 1895,
      "explanation": "After the patches land, resolveConflicts repairs references: removed ids are unbound, added ids are rebound, and updated ids are rebound only when their patch touched a binding-related property. Its updater adjusts affected neighbour revision counters by direction, adding one on forward replay and subtracting one on backward replay, unless that element's version was already changed; it then computes deltas for the before/after affected elements and squashes them back into the current ElementsDelta so the binding repairs are part of the entry."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/delta.ts",
      "symbol": "checkForVisibleDifference",
      "line": 1819,
      "explanation": "This comparator first reduces each side to what can actually paint: missing or deleted elements become null, and an empty text label with no arrow bindings and whose container is not an arrow becomes null. Painting elements are compared property by property while ignoring index, version, versionNonce, updated, and created metadata, and comparing boundElements as type:id references after dropping references to empty labels. Consequently, deleting or restoring an empty label and its reference is invisible, while a nonempty label, an arrow gap, or layout-induced geometry change is visible."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/fractionalIndex.ts",
      "symbol": "orderByFractionalIndex",
      "line": 150,
      "explanation": "This sorter restores stacking order by comparing fractional indices, breaking equal-index ties by element id. If either element lacks a valid fractional index, it returns 1 so the existing array order is defensively retained. ElementsDelta.reorderElements uses it before synchronizing moved indices."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/delta.ts",
      "symbol": "applyLatestChanges",
      "line": 1309,
      "explanation": "ElementsDelta.applyLatestChanges refreshes the entry from the current scene after replay. Its modifier copies each partial key from the appropriate previous or next element, except boundElements, which keeps the delta's postprocessed attachment list. Only ids whose deleted and inserted partials still differ remain in the rewritten buckets, so per-object patches that no longer represent a change are dropped; deltas are redistributed afterward because isDeleted may have changed."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/store.ts",
      "symbol": "maybeClone",
      "line": 761,
      "explanation": "StoreSnapshot.maybeClone returns this same snapshot instance when neither elements nor observed app state changed. It enables shouldCompareHashes only for the deferred CaptureUpdateAction.EVENTUALLY mode, so repeated comparisons in that mode are suppressed by the last changed element or app-state hash; IMMEDIATELY and NEVER rely on the normal structural/version checks."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/element/src/store.ts",
      "symbol": "emitDurableIncrement",
      "line": 216,
      "explanation": "Store.emitDurableIncrement is the publishing step for undoable durable updates. When a delta argument is supplied, as History.perform does through scheduleMicroAction, it reuses that exact StoreDelta instance instead of recalculating from the previous and next snapshots. It emits a DurableIncrement on both durable and public increment emitters only when the delta is nonempty; preserving object identity lets History.record recognize the replayed HistoryDelta and skip recording it again."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "Undo starts from the stored inverse HistoryDelta and calls HistoryDelta.applyTo. That method invokes ElementsDelta.applyTo with the previous store snapshot as fallback and with version/versionNonce excluded. ElementsDelta.applyTo builds the per-bucket enactor with createApplier; createApplier resolves elements via createGetter, applies patches, and infers replay direction from the first comparable before/after version pair. After the buckets are applied, resolveConflicts unbinds removed objects, rebinds added or binding-touching objects, adjusts affected neighbours by +1 for forward or -1 for backward, and squashes those repairs into the entry. ElementsDelta.applyTo then calls reorderElements, which uses orderByFractionalIndex only when a z-index difference was detected, redraws text boxes and bound arrows, and finally uses checkForVisibleDifference to decide whether the applied result actually paints. History.perform combines that with the app-state answer, refreshes the entry through HistoryDelta.applyLatestChanges and ElementsDelta.applyLatestChanges, clones the next snapshot with StoreSnapshot.maybeClone, schedules a micro action carrying the already-computed delta, and finally calls the store's emitDurableIncrement, which reuses the supplied delta instance. The App's durable-increment listener passes that delta to History.record, but record immediately returns for a HistoryDelta, so the replayed update is not pushed onto the undo stack again; History.perform explicitly pushes the inverse of the entry onto the opposite stack and emits onHistoryChanged only once in its finally block. The replay directory is decided by createApplier's version comparison: prevElement.version > nextElement.version means backward, otherwise forward, with forward as the fallback. resolveConflicts then applies that direction to repaired neighbours, decrementing their versions on a backward replay and incrementing them on a forward replay; because history application deliberately excludes version and versionNonce, the applied element is derived from the current version plus one, so this path normally resolves forward and repaired neighbours are bumped rather than version-rewound. One undo press can skip several entries because History.perform keeps popping while the combined element/app-state visible-change answer is false; an empty label's deletion, restoration, or font change compares as nothing-to-nothing in checkForVisibleDifference, so those entries are traversed and pushed to the redo stack without stopping the loop. Re-sorting is skipped entirely inside ElementsDelta.reorderElements when flags.containsZindexDifference is false, which happens when no applied patch changed index and no element had to be resurrected from the snapshot; only then is orderByFractionalIndex avoided. Finally, the published update is not re-recorded because Store.emitDurableIncrement reuses the HistoryDelta instance supplied by History.perform, and History.record rejects both empty deltas and every HistoryDelta instance."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/components/App.tsx",
      "symbol": "savePointer",
      "line": 11325,
      "explanation": "Editor-core cursor bridge. It first suppresses updates while a pan gesture is active, and while interaction is disabled unless the current tool is one explicitly enabled through interaction settings (so a presenter's laser can keep broadcasting). It also suppresses falsy coordinates, so a client x or y of 0 is rejected rather than sent. It converts client coordinates through viewportCoordsToSceneCoords, builds a CollaboratorPointer with x/y and tool set to \"laser\" when the active tool is laser (otherwise \"pointer\"), then calls the embedding host's props.onPointerUpdate with the pointer, button state, and gesture.pointers. The NaN branch has only a comment and does not return."
    },
    {
      "path": "excalidraw-app/collab/Portal.tsx",
      "symbol": "broadcastMouseLocation",
      "line": 202,
      "explanation": "Transport wrapper for cursor presence. If the socket is connected, it builds a MOUSE_LOCATION payload containing the sender socket id, pointer (x/y/tool), button defaulted to \"up\", the current selectedElementIds from app state, and the local username. It then calls _broadcastSocketData(data, true), which JSON-encodes the payload, encrypts it with encryptData and the room key, and emits it through the volatile socket event. Delivery is therefore encrypted and fire-and-forget rather than part of the synced scene."
    },
    {
      "path": "excalidraw-app/collab/Collab.tsx",
      "symbol": "updateCollaborator",
      "line": 912,
      "explanation": "Receiving-side presence merge. It copies the current collaborators map, merges the partial Collaborator updates for one socket id over any existing entry, marks whether this is the current user, and, for the current user, seeds the entry with the local username because a client never receives its own socket broadcasts. It stores the new map and pushes it to the editor via excalidrawAPI.updateScene({ collaborators }). Both MOUSE_LOCATION and IDLE_STATUS handlers feed this method."
    },
    {
      "path": "packages/excalidraw/clients.ts",
      "symbol": "renderRemoteCursors",
      "line": 56,
      "explanation": "Interactive-layer painter for remote participants. It iterates the viewport-coordinate map, offsets by the canvas offset, clamps each pointer into the canvas rectangle, and determines isOutOfBounds. A participant is treated as inactive when out of bounds or when their userState is IDLE or AWAY; in both cases it sets globalAlpha to 0.3 and draws the alternate faded arrow shape. It also draws the pressed-button ring, optional speaking outline, and the name tag, but the name tag is drawn only when the position is not out of bounds. Thus an out-of-bounds pointer is clamped and loses its label, while an in-bounds idle pointer keeps its position/label but is faded."
    },
    {
      "path": "excalidraw-app/collab/Collab.tsx",
      "symbol": "onPointerMove",
      "line": 832,
      "explanation": "Document-wide activity listener registered on EVENT.POINTER_MOVE. Movement clears any existing idle timeout and starts a new IDLE_THRESHOLD countdown to reportIdle; it also lazily starts the ACTIVE_THRESHOLD interval that repeatedly calls reportActive, ensuring the periodic still-here heartbeat runs while the user is moving."
    },
    {
      "path": "excalidraw-app/collab/Collab.tsx",
      "symbol": "onVisibilityChange",
      "line": 848,
      "explanation": "Page visibility handler. When document.hidden is true it clears both idleTimeoutId and activeIntervalId (setting them to null) and reports UserIdleState.AWAY through onIdleStateChange, which Portal.broadcastIdleChange sends as a volatile, encrypted IDLE_STATUS message containing socketId, userState, and username. When the page becomes visible again it re-arms the idle timeout and active interval, then immediately reports UserIdleState.ACTIVE."
    }
  ],
  "flow": "Cursor path: local canvas pointer movement reaches handleCanvasPointerMove, which calls App.savePointer(event.clientX, event.clientY, button). savePointer refuses to hand anything to the host when a pan is active, when interaction is disabled and the active tool is not one of the enabled tools, or when either coordinate is falsy (including 0); a NaN result is not explicitly rejected. Otherwise it converts client coordinates to scene coordinates, chooses a CollaboratorPointer whose tool is \"laser\" only when the active tool is laser, and invokes props.onPointerUpdate with the pointer, button, and gesture.pointers. Collab.onPointerUpdate is a throttled wrapper (CURSOR_SYNC_TIMEOUT) that filters multi-touch by broadcasting only when payload.pointersMap.size < 2; this is where multi-finger gestures are filtered out. It then calls Portal.broadcastMouseLocation, which adds socketId, pointer, button, selectedElementIds, and username, and sends a volatile MOUSE_LOCATION message through _broadcastSocketData, where it is JSON-encoded and encrypted with the room key. Peers receive the decrypted MOUSE_LOCATION in Collab's client-broadcast handler, extract pointer/button/username/selectedElementIds (with a legacy socketID fallback), and call updateCollaborator. That method merges the partial presence into the participant map and pushes the whole map into the editor with updateScene. InteractiveCanvas converts each collaborator's scene pointer back to viewport coordinates and renderRemoteCursors paints the arrow and name tag on the interactive layer. Laser strokes are distinguished in the same pointer payload by pointer.tool === \"laser\" (set by savePointer); the normal remote-arrow painter still draws the cursor, while LaserTrails.updateCollabTrails separately checks collaborator.pointer.tool === \"laser\" together with button down/up to build the remote laser trail. Activity path: initializeIdleDetector attaches document pointermove and visibilitychange listeners. onPointerMove resets the IDLE_THRESHOLD countdown and ensures the ACTIVE_THRESHOLD reportActive interval is running. If movement stops, reportIdle fires after 60 seconds, broadcasts UserIdleState.IDLE, and clears the active interval. If the tab becomes hidden, onVisibilityChange clears both the idle timeout and the active interval and broadcasts UserIdleState.AWAY. Receivers merge that userState through updateCollaborator; renderRemoteCursors treats IDLE or AWAY, like an out-of-bounds pointer, as inactive, setting globalAlpha 0.3 and drawing the alternate faded arrow. Out-of-bounds coordinates are additionally clamped into the visible canvas and their name tag is omitted, whereas an in-bounds inactive participant remains at the normal position with a faded cursor and, if present, still gets the username label. When the tab is shown again, onVisibilityChange re-arms both timers and immediately reports ACTIVE, unfading the user. The local participant's own map entry is seeded with the local display name in both setCollaborators and updateCollaborator because the local client never receives its own broadcasts; without seeding, the local entry would have no username in the collaborator map."
}
